1. **A ferritic stainless steel having excellent vibration damping capability**, consisting essentially of, in mass percent:

- C: 0.001 to 0.030%;
- Si: 0.05 to 2.0%;
- Mn: 0.05 to 2.0%;
- P: 0.040% or less;
- S: 0.010% or less;
- Cr: 10.0 to 25.0%;
- Ni: 0 to 1.0%;
- Al: 0.005 to 3.0%;
- N: 0.001 to 0.030%;
- at least one stabilizing element selected from Ti: 0.02 to 0.50% and Nb: 0.02 to 0.80%;
- optionally at least one element selected from Mo: 0.05 to 3.0%, Cu: 0.05 to 1.5%, V: 0.01 to 0.30%, B: 0.0002 to 0.005%, and rare earth metal: 0.001 to 0.10%; and
- the balance Fe and unavoidable impurities,

wherein the steel has a microstructure comprising **90 area% or more ferrite phase**, with martensite and austenite together being **5 area% or less**, and wherein carbide, nitride, and carbonitride precipitates are dispersed so that an average equivalent circle diameter thereof is **1.0 μm or less**;

wherein the ferrite phase has an average crystal grain size of **10 to 200 μm** and a crystallographic texture in which a total area fraction of grains having a {100} plane within 15° of a sheet surface is **20% or more**;

wherein the steel exhibits magnetic properties comprising a coercive force of **200 A/m or less**, a maximum relative magnetic permeability of **1,000 or more**, and a magnetic flux density B50 of **1.0 T or more**; and

wherein, when measured at room temperature under a bending or tensile vibration condition at a strain amplitude of 1×10⁻⁵ to 1×10⁻³, the steel exhibits an internal friction or loss factor corresponding to **tan δ of 0.005 or more**, thereby providing vibration damping capability while maintaining corrosion resistance characteristic of ferritic stainless steel.

A ferritic stainless steel material for vibration damping, comprising, in mass percent:

- C: 0.001 to 0.030%;
- Si: 0.05 to 2.00%;
- Mn: 0.05 to 1.00%;
- P: not more than 0.050%;
- S: not more than 0.010%;
- Cr: 10.5 to 20.0%;
- Ni: not more than 0.60%;
- Al: 0.005 to 0.200%;
- N: 0.001 to 0.030%;
- optionally one or more of Ti: 0.01 to 0.50%, Nb: 0.01 to 0.60%, and Mo: 0.05 to 2.00%; and
- the balance being Fe and unavoidable impurities,

wherein the steel material has a ferrite single-phase matrix, an average ferrite crystal grain size of 0.3 to 3.0 mm, and a residual magnetic flux density of not more than 45 mT.

The ferritic stainless steel material is obtained by subjecting a steel having the above chemical composition to hot working, cold working as required, and final annealing under conditions effective to coarsen ferrite grains while maintaining the ferrite single-phase structure. By controlling the ferrite crystal grain size within the range of 0.3 to 3.0 mm and reducing the residual magnetic flux density to 45 mT or less, internal friction is increased and vibration damping performance is improved while corrosion resistance and formability characteristic of ferritic stainless steel are retained.

The material is suitable for vibration-damping members, structural panels, covers, housings, brackets, and other components required to suppress vibration or noise.
